% title "Certification checks";
% layout 'default';

<h2><%= title %></h2>
<p class="lead">
  Every submitted release runs through 13 automated checks before it can publish.
  This page explains what each one looks for and how to write your plugin so it
  passes.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
  Failing a check doesn't necessarily stop your plugin from publishing -- see
  <a href="#tiers">how the tiers work</a> below.
</div>

<h3 id="tiers">How the tiers work</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Required</strong> -- fail any one of these and your release stays
    in "changes requested" and never publishes, no matter what else passes.</li>
  <li><strong>Certification-gating</strong> -- these don't block publishing, but
    your release only reaches the <span class="badge text-bg-success">CERTIFIED</span>
    badge if all of them pass too. Fail one and it still publishes, just at
    <span class="badge text-bg-warning">STRUCTURAL</span>.</li>
  <li><strong>Informational</strong> -- recorded on your release's technical
    report, but don't block publishing or affect your certification tier.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  You can see the result of every check for your own release on its version
  page, under "Technical report".
</p>

<h3 class="mt-5">Required checks</h3>
<p class="text-muted">Fail one of these and your release never publishes.</p>

<h4><code>perl_syntax</code></h4>
<p>
  Every <code>.pm</code> file in your release must compile (<code>perl -cw</code>)
  against the Koha version you declared as <code>minimum_version</code>. Run
  <code>perl -cw YourPlugin.pm</code> yourself against that Koha version before
  tagging a release.
</p>

<h4><code>manifest_completeness</code></h4>
<p>
  Your plugin's <code>$metadata</code> hash must declare both <code>version</code>
  and <code>license</code>:
</p>
<pre><code>our $metadata = {
    name            =&gt; 'My Plugin',
    description     =&gt; 'What it does',
    author          =&gt; 'Your Name',
    minimum_version =&gt; '23.05',
    version         =&gt; '1.0.0',
    license         =&gt; 'GPL-3.0',
};</code></pre>

<h4><code>dependency_allowlist</code></h4>
<p>
  Your code is scanned for patterns the store won't allow in a submitted
  plugin, since these can't be reviewed automatically and are common vectors
  for something going badly wrong on a library's live system:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>system()</code>, <code>exec()</code>, backticks, or <code>qx</code></li>
  <li><code>use</code>-ing a networking module directly (<code>IO::Socket</code>,
    <code>Net::*</code>, <code>LWP</code>, <code>HTTP::Tiny</code>) -- use Koha's
    own HTTP client wrappers instead</li>
  <li><code>open()</code>-ing an absolute filesystem path</li>
  <li>a string referencing <code>../</code> to escape your plugin's own directory</li>
</ul>
<p class="text-muted">
  This is a static analysis of real parsed Perl syntax, not a text search --
  mentioning one of these in a comment or string won't false-positive.
</p>

<h3 class="mt-5">Certification-gating checks</h3>
<p class="text-muted">
  These don't block publishing, but all of them need to pass for your release
  to reach the <span class="badge text-bg-success">CERTIFIED</span> tier.
</p>

<h4><code>perl_critic</code></h4>
<p>
  Every <code>.pm</code> file is run through Koha's own Perl::Critic policy set.
  Run it yourself locally against your plugin's files before submitting.
</p>

<h4><code>docs_presence</code></h4>
<p>
  Include a <code>README</code>, <code>README.md</code>, <code>Development.md</code>,
  <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code>, or a <code>docs/</code> directory somewhere in
  your repository.
</p>

<h4><code>readme_presence</code></h4>
<p>
  Specifically: GitHub needs to recognize a README for your repository (the
  same one it'd show on your repo's own front page -- <code>README.md</code>,
  <code>README.rst</code>, or a bare <code>README</code> all count). It's shown
  in full on your plugin's page here, so it's worth keeping it current.
</p>

<h4><code>changelog_format</code></h4>
<p>
  Include a <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> (or <code>CHANGES.md</code>) with at
  least one heading shaped like a
  <a href="https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Keep a Changelog</a>
  or <a href="https://common-changelog.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Common Changelog</a>
  version entry:
</p>
<pre><code>## [1.2.0] - 2026-01-01

### Added
- A new widget for doing the thing.

### Fixed
- The thing that was broken.</code></pre>
<p>
  A bare <code>## v1.2.0</code> heading passes too -- this check isn't full
  spec validation (it doesn't check section names, dates, or an "Unreleased"
  section), just "is there a real, dated version entry here." That same
  heading is what powers the "What's new in this version" summary shown on
  your plugin's page, so passing this check also means that summary will
  actually show up.
</p>

<h4><code>tests_presence</code></h4>
<p>
  Include at least one <code>t/*.t</code> file in your tagged source repository.
  (Your <code>.kpz</code> release archive itself doesn't need to contain
  tests -- this check looks at the tagged commit on GitHub, not the archive.)
</p>

<h4><code>translatable_templates</code></h4>
<p>
  Any <code>.tt</code> template rendering visible text (<code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>,
  <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, etc.) should mark that
  text for translation:
</p>
<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;[% t('Hello, world') %]&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre>

<h4><code>plugin_template_wrapper</code></h4>
<p>
  Every <code>.tt</code> template should include Koha's plugin template
  wrapper, currently <code>doc-head-close.inc</code>:
</p>
<pre><code>[% INCLUDE 'doc-head-close.inc' %]</code></pre>

<h4><code>hardcoded_credentials</code></h4>
<p>
  Don't assign a literal secret to a credential-shaped variable name (e.g.
  <code>api_key</code>, <code>password</code>) anywhere in your code, and don't
  commit a PEM private key or an AWS access key. Use Koha's own configuration
  mechanisms for anything a library needs to supply themselves.
</p>

<h3 class="mt-5">Informational checks</h3>
<p class="text-muted">
  Recorded on your release's technical report, but don't block publishing or
  affect your certification tier.
</p>

<h4><code>koha_max_version</code></h4>
<p>
  Whether your <code>$metadata</code> declares a <code>maximum_version</code>
  in addition to <code>minimum_version</code>. Worth setting if you know your
  plugin doesn't work against newer Koha releases yet.
</p>

<h4><code>gpg_signed_tag</code></h4>
<p>
  Whether the release tag itself was GPG-signed on GitHub
  (<code>git tag -s v1.2.0</code>). This is about your own tag's authenticity,
  separate from the store's own signing of the published <code>.kpz</code> --
  see the <a href="/verification-key">verification key</a> page for that.
</p>
